---
// Lesson 1.4 — Movement I: Input, Direction, Vector
// First-introduction lesson: FULL scaffold (walkthrough level 1).
//
// The old version of this lesson handed students three one-line snippets from
// foreign machines (forklift / elevator / conveyor) and a callout of thirteen
// questions, then expected them to assemble movement from that. The forklift
// snippet in particular — `float steeringIntent = Input.GetAxis("Horizontal");`
// — taught a declare-and-initialize shape that is the WRONG shape here, with
// nothing said about why. Students mirrored it, declared their input variable
// inside Update(), and it re-initialized every frame. The section that carried
// those snippets has been removed; the walkthrough replaces it and the scope
// rule from 1.3 is re-invoked at the exact line where it matters.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';
import { renderWalkthrough, renderAskBanner } from '../../lib/walkthrough';
import { renderDecompose } from '../../lib/decompose';

// The two recurring questions, applied to movement. Level 1, so every part
// is given with what it does AND where to read about it — the student has
// never split a problem before, so the splitting is demonstrated, not asked
// for. The parts deliberately match the walkthrough's three steps: the split
// is not a warm-up that gets thrown away, it IS the plan the build follows.
const DECOMPOSE = {
  big:
    'Move the character when I press the arrow keys. That is the whole feature — and as a ' +
    'single problem it is far too big to start typing.',
  parts: [
    {
      name: 'Find out which way the player is pushing',
      does: 'Ask the keyboard for two numbers: one for left/right, one for up/down. Each comes back between -1 and 1. Store them in fields on the class, so they survive the frame they were read in.',
      doc: 'Input.GetAxis and the declare-vs-assign rule you met in 1.3, both in the quick reference.',
      href: '../docs/reference/unity-csharp-quickref.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Turn those two numbers into one direction',
      does: 'A pair of numbers is awkward to move by. Combine them into a single Vector2 — one value that means "this far in this direction", which is what the movement step wants.',
      doc: 'Vector2, in the quick reference.',
      href: '../docs/reference/unity-csharp-quickref.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Move the player by that direction, every frame',
      does: 'Offset the position by direction x speed x the time since the last frame. Multiplying by that time is what keeps the speed in units per second rather than units per frame.',
      doc: 'transform.Translate and Time.deltaTime, in the quick reference.',
      href: '../docs/reference/unity-csharp-quickref.html',
    },
  ],
  why:
    'Three parts, and notice that only one of them is the part you would have thought of first. ' +
    '"Move the player" sounds like one action; it is a read, a build and an offset. Nearly every ' +
    'feature in this game splits the same way — something you find out, something you work out ' +
    'from it, and something you do with it.',
  sizeTest:
    'The test for a finished split: could you look this part up on its own, right now, in one ' +
    'search? "Move the player" cannot be looked up. "Get keyboard input as a number" can.',
};

const WALK = {
  ask: 'how do I move an object in Unity?',
  file: 'Assets/Scripts/PlayerController.cs',
  intro: 'You will build two-axis movement — the kind you steer with WASD or the arrow keys — and every line of it is given and explained below.',
  level: 1,
  steps: [
    {
      title: 'Open the class and add the speed knob',
      lead: 'PlayerController should already exist on the Player with two empty methods. The first thing it needs is a number that says how fast to move.',
      lines: [
        { code: 'using UnityEngine;', what: 'Same as last lesson — gives you `MonoBehaviour`, `Input`, `Vector2`, `Time` and `Transform`.' },
        { code: '', what: '' },
        { code: 'public class PlayerController : MonoBehaviour', what: 'The class that is already attached to your Player. Do not rename it — Unity finds it by this name.' },
        { code: '{', what: '' },
        { code: '    [SerializeField] private float moveSpeed = 5f;', what: 'How many units the player travels in one second. `[SerializeField]` exposes it in the Inspector so you can tune it live without touching code.' },
      ],
    },
    {
      title: 'Declare the fields that remember the input',
      lead: 'This is last lesson\'s rule doing real work. Read the two comments carefully — this exact step is where movement code usually goes wrong.',
      lines: [
        { code: '    private float inputX;', what: 'Stores the left/right key direction as a number: `-1` for left, `0` for nothing, `1` for right. It sits in the **class body**, so it belongs to the Player and survives from one frame to the next.' },
        { code: '    private float inputY;', what: 'The same thing for up/down: `-1`, `0`, or `1`.' , why: '**Why here and not inside Update()?** If you declare `inputX` inside `Update()`, it is created fresh 60 times a second and thrown away each time. Nothing after that frame could ever see it. Declaring it here means `Update()` **assigns** to a variable that already exists — exactly the declare/assign split from 1.3.' },
        { code: '', what: '' },
        { code: '    void Update()', what: 'The method Unity calls every frame. All three of the remaining steps happen inside it.' },
        { code: '    {', what: '' },
      ],
    },
    {
      title: 'Read the keyboard',
      lead: 'Ask Unity what the player is pressing right now, and store the answer in the two fields you just declared.',
      lines: [
        { code: '        inputX = Input.GetAxis("Horizontal");', what: 'Reads the virtual axis called `"Horizontal"` and stores the result in `inputX`. Notice there is **no `float` in front of it** — the variable already exists, so this line only gives it a value.', why: 'Writing `float inputX = ...` here instead would create a *second, temporary* variable that shadows the field, dies at the end of the frame, and leaves the real field untouched. That is the single most common bug in this lesson.' },
        { code: '        inputY = Input.GetAxis("Vertical");', what: 'The same read for up/down. The axis name in quotes must be spelled exactly `"Vertical"` — capitals matter, and a typo throws an error rather than failing quietly.' },
        { code: '', what: '' },
        { code: '        Vector2 movement = new Vector2(', what: 'Builds one value that holds both directions at once. This one **is** declared inside `Update()`, and that is correct — it is built from this frame\'s input and used two lines later. Nothing next frame needs it.' },
        { code: '            inputX * moveSpeed * Time.deltaTime,', what: 'The horizontal distance to travel this frame: direction × speed × how long the frame lasted. The commas matter — this is the first of two arguments.' },
        { code: '            inputY * moveSpeed * Time.deltaTime);', what: 'The vertical distance, same recipe. The closing `);` finishes the `Vector2` and the line.' },
      ],
    },
    {
      title: 'Apply the movement',
      lead: 'You now hold a displacement — "move this far from where I am." Hand it to the Transform.',
      lines: [
        { code: '        transform.Translate(movement);', what: 'Offsets the Player\'s position by that displacement. `transform` is the Player\'s own position/rotation/scale, available on every GameObject without you declaring it.' },
        { code: '    }', what: 'Closes `Update()`. One more `}` closes the class.' },
      ],
    },
  ],
  // The reference is deliberately NOT the same shape as the walkthrough above
  // it. Both are correct; this one works out the distance-per-frame once and
  // multiplies the whole direction by it, where the walkthrough scaled each
  // component. So the file says on its own terms whether it was built or
  // pasted — and a student comparing the two has to read for meaning rather
  // than diff for text, which is the point. What must NOT change is anything a
  // later lesson builds on: inputX, inputY, moveSpeed and the Vector2 all stay,
  // because 1.5 is about that vector's length and 1.6 reads those two inputs.
  finalFile: `using UnityEngine;

public class PlayerController : MonoBehaviour
{
    [SerializeField] private float moveSpeed = 5f;

    private float inputX;
    private float inputY;

    void Update()
    {
        inputX = Input.GetAxis("Horizontal");
        inputY = Input.GetAxis("Vertical");

        float stepThisFrame = moveSpeed * Time.deltaTime;
        Vector2 movement = new Vector2(inputX, inputY) * stepThisFrame;

        transform.Translate(movement);
    }
}`,
  finalNote:
    'Compare this against your own file. Two things are worth checking closely: that `inputX` and `inputY` are declared **up in the class body** and only assigned inside `Update()`, and that both displacement lines are multiplied by `Time.deltaTime`.',
};

const body = `<h1>1.4 — Movement I: Input, Direction, Vector</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    In a game, movement looks effortless. In code it is an algorithm, and today you build it:
    <strong>read the player's input → turn it into a distance → move the character by that
    distance</strong>. Three steps, one of them per frame. Every line is given to you below
    with an explanation of what it does — nothing here is a puzzle to solve on your own.
</p>

<div class="callout info">
    <strong>Where you are starting from.</strong> Open <code>Assets/Scripts/PlayerController.cs</code>.
    It is a shell — a class with two empty methods. Your character has a Transform and nothing
    else: no physics body, no collider. Everything your character does from here on, you write.
    That is the whole design of this course.
</div>

${renderAskBanner('how do I move an object in Unity?', 1)}

${renderDecompose('1.4', DECOMPOSE, 1)}

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity discovery">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Read the Docs</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~12 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        You are about to write one of these into your code. Read all four before you choose —
        they are genuinely different tools, and the choice changes how the game feels.
    </p>

    <table>
        <tr><th>Unity API</th><th>Returns</th><th>Behaviour</th></tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>Input.GetAxis("Horizontal")</code></td>
            <td><code>float</code> (−1.0 to 1.0)</td>
            <td><strong>Smooth ramp.</strong> Gradually accelerates toward −1 or +1, then coasts back to 0 when you let go. Gives movement a little weight.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>Input.GetAxisRaw("Horizontal")</code></td>
            <td><code>float</code> (−1, 0, or 1)</td>
            <td><strong>Instant snap.</strong> Exactly −1, 0 or 1 the moment a key changes, with no smoothing. Stops the instant you let go.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>Input.GetKey(KeyCode.D)</code></td>
            <td><code>bool</code></td>
            <td><strong>Binary state.</strong> True while the physical D key is held; false otherwise. Always the same key, on every keyboard.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>transform.Translate(vector)</code></td>
            <td><code>void</code></td>
            <td><strong>Offsets the position by a vector.</strong> A <em>relative</em> step: "move this far from wherever I am right now."</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <p>
        Notice that <code>"Horizontal"</code> and <code>"Vertical"</code> are quoted strings,
        not key names. They are <strong>virtual axes</strong>, defined in
        Edit → Project Settings → Input Manager. Open that window and read what each one is
        bound to — you may be surprised how much is wired up already.
    </p>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic: Evaluating Multiple Ways to Solve a Problem</h3>
        <ol>
<li>For a driving game, or a character with heavy momentum, which gives the better feel — <code>GetAxis</code> or <code>GetAxisRaw</code>? Why?</li>
<li>For a precision grid puzzle, or a fast arcade game where you must stop on a dime, why might <code>GetAxisRaw</code> be better?</li>
<li>What happens if a player wants to use the arrow keys, or a USB controller, instead of WASD? Why is asking for the virtual axis <code>"Horizontal"</code> more flexible than hardcoding <code>KeyCode.D</code>?</li>
</ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
${renderWalkthrough(WALK)}

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Test It, Then Break It</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~22 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        Save your script, return to Unity, press Play, and hold WASD or the arrow keys. Your
        character should glide in all four directions. Now prove you know why.
    </p>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Bug Hunt: Testing Assumptions</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Delete <code>* Time.deltaTime</code> from both displacement lines. What happens when you <em>tap</em> a direction key? Why does the character cross the entire map in a single frame?</li>
            <li>Set your <code>moveSpeed</code> knob to <code>0</code> in the Inspector while the game runs. What happens? Set it to <code>30</code>. Describe the difference in one sentence.</li>
            <li>Swap the two lines inside <code>new Vector2(...)</code> — put the vertical calculation where the horizontal belongs, and vice versa. What does the character do now, and why?</li>
            <li><strong>Now move your input declaration.</strong> Cut <code>private float inputX;</code> from the class body and put <code>float inputX = Input.GetAxis("Horizontal");</code> inside <code>Update()</code> instead. Does the character still move? Explain what happened to the variable, using the words <em>declare</em> and <em>assign</em>.</li>
            <li><strong>Now the interesting one.</strong> Walk your character straight into a wall and keep pushing. What happens? Look back at the component list from <a href="1.2.html">1.2</a> — what does the Player <em>not</em> have that would be needed to stop it?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="callout trap">
        <strong>Keep that last answer.</strong> Walking through walls is not a mistake in your
        code — <code>transform.Translate</code> moves your character by editing coordinates
        directly, so it never asks the physics engine anything. It will happily put you inside
        a wall, because nothing ever told it not to. Whether that matters depends entirely on
        the game you are making. In the next lesson you will compare every way Unity offers to
        move an object, and decide which one this game actually needs.
    </div>


    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>State the three sub-problems of character movement: input → vector → translation</li>
            <li><strong>Point at your own file and say which lines declare and which lines assign</strong></li>
            <li>Explain the difference between <code>Input.GetAxis</code>, <code>Input.GetAxisRaw</code>, and <code>Input.GetKey</code></li>
            <li>Explain why multiplying by <code>Time.deltaTime</code> is necessary</li>
            <li>Find and tune your speed knob in the Inspector while the game runs</li>
            <li>Explain why your character can walk through a wall right now</li>
            <li>Commit and push your new movement code to your GitHub fork</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <p>Need a deeper explanation? These pages cover the same concepts:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-01/index.html">Unit 1: Setup &amp; Movement</a> — input, vectors, and translation in depth</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/reference/unity-csharp-quickref.html">Unity + C# Quick Reference</a> — input and vector functions</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/ask.html">How do I…? index</a> — find this lesson again by the question you were asking</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Next: <a href="1.5.html">1.5 — Movement II: Speed, deltaTime &amp; Tuning</a>, where you compare all four ways to move an object and pick the one this game needs.</p>
</div>`;
